        <div id="battery-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Battery Capacity: Amp-Hours, Volts and Kilowatt-Hours
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spec sheets list batteries in amp-hours, which is only half the number. Capacity in watt-hours is volts multiplied by amp-hours, and that is the figure you can compare across brands. A 56V 10.0Ah EGO pack holds about 560 watt-hours. A Greenworks 80V 16.0Ah pack holds about 1,280. Add every pack in the kit and you get the mower's total energy, which is the closest thing an electric mower has to tank size.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below uses that arithmetic on each kit's included batteries. These are nominal figures from voltage and amp-hour ratings, not measured capacity, and real usable energy will be somewhat lower once the battery management system reserves its margins. They are still the fairest way to compare a four-pack EGO with a six-pack Greenworks, because the brands' own acreage ratings are measured under conditions they do not publish.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mower</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Included Packs</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Nominal Capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated Acres</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">EGO ZT4204L (42 in)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 x 56V 10.0Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.2 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 to 2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">EGO ZT4215S (42 in, e-Steer)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 x 10.0Ah + 4 x 6.0Ah, 56V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.5 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">EGO ZT5207L (52 in)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 x 56V 12.0Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4.0 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3 to 4</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Greenworks MZ606 (60 in)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 x 20Ah + 4 x 8Ah, 60V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4.3 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Over 4</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Greenworks MZ5481 (54 in)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 x 16Ah + 4 x 6Ah, 80V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4.5 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 4</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. First, the manufacturers' acreage claims land at roughly one kilowatt-hour per acre, give or take. That is a useful sanity check on any listing: if a kit with 2 kWh of batteries claims 4 acres, the claim assumes short, dry grass and a flat, open lawn. Second, the jump from a 42-inch kit to a 52- or 54-inch kit roughly doubles the battery, not just the deck. That is why the bigger machines feel so much less stressful to own; the battery is sized for the deck rather than barely covering it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Voltage on its own does not make a mower better. An 80V system can deliver the same power at lower current than a 56V or 60V system, which helps efficiency a little, but total watt-hours and the motor controller matter more. Do not pay extra for a higher voltage number if the kit ships with fewer watt-hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            For context, the Cub Cadet ZT1 42E, which is sold through Cub Cadet dealers and big-box stores rather than Amazon, lists a 56V, 60Ah, 3,000 watt-hour battery and Cub Cadet rates it at up to 2 acres per charge. That fits the same roughly 1 to 1.5 kWh per acre pattern, and it is a reasonable alternative if you have a Cub Cadet dealer nearby for service.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="acreage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Real Acreage per Charge: Why the Rating Is a Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every acreage figure on a box is a best case. The manufacturers do not publish their test conditions, but owners commonly report that thick, damp spring growth can cut range by a third or more compared with a mid-summer trim. Grass length is the biggest single factor. Cutting four inches down to three takes far less energy than cutting seven inches down to three, because the blade motors draw more current to hold blade speed through heavy grass, and that current is what drains the pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed is the second factor. Driving at the full 8 mph on a dense lawn forces the blade motors to work harder per second and can leave an uneven cut anyway. Slowing to 5 or 6 mph in heavy grass usually costs less battery per acre than the extra pass you would otherwise make. Bagging is the third. Pushing clippings up a chute into a bagger takes more airflow than mulching or side discharge, and high-lift bagging blades draw more power. If you plan to bag, assume the rated acreage drops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan on 70% of the rating</h3>

          <p>
            A practical way to buy is to take the manufacturer's acreage figure, multiply it by 0.7, and make sure your lawn fits inside that number. On that basis the ZT4204L is a 1 to 1.4 acre mower, the ZT5207L handles about 2 to 2.8 acres, and the two Greenworks MaximusZ kits cover roughly 2.8 acres. If your lawn is right on the line, buy the next size up. Running a battery mower to a dead stop every week is the fastest way to shorten pack life and the surest way to finish the last corner with a push mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that the acreage number refers to mowed area, not lot size. A 2-acre lot with a house, driveway, garden beds and a tree line may only have 1.3 acres of grass. Measure the turf itself with a satellite map tool before you shop, and you may find a 42-inch kit is enough after all. Our <a href="/what-size-riding-mower-do-i-need/">riding mower sizing guide</a> walks through that measurement.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rule-of-Thumb Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Battery per rated acre, across these kits</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">70%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Share of rated acreage to plan on</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8 mph</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Listed top speed, every model here</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.5-4.5 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cut height range, every model here</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Charge Time and Where You Plug It In
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every mower on this page charges from a standard 120V household outlet. That sounds like a non-issue until you look at the charger wattage. EGO's Z6 kits ship with a 1600W charger, and both Greenworks MaximusZ kits include a 1.5kW charger. A 1,600 watt load is about 13 amps at 120V, which is most of a standard 15-amp garage circuit. Run a shop vac, a space heater or a freezer on the same circuit and you can trip the breaker partway through a charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best fix is simple: plug the charger into a 20-amp circuit with nothing else on it, or at least make sure nothing else heavy runs while it charges. Do not use a thin household extension cord. If you need a cord, use a short, heavy-gauge outdoor-rated one, and keep the charger off the bare floor where it can sit in water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long a recharge actually takes</h3>

          <p>
            Published figures vary with the kit. One EGO retailer lists the ZT4215S at about 2.5 hours with its included charger, and another retailer lists the ZT5207L at about four hours to charge its six 12.0Ah packs. Greenworks says the MZ606 recharges in under 4 hours, Tractor Supply lists under 3.5, and a Greenworks listing for an 80V MaximusZ kit quotes under 2.5 hours. Cub Cadet says its ZT1 42E takes about 4 hours. For most owners the practical answer is the same: charge it overnight, mow in the morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a battery zero turn cannot do is mow 5 acres today if it is rated for 3. There is no quick refuel. If you regularly need more than one charge in a day, the options are a second set of packs (expensive), a larger kit, or a gas machine. On EGO kits, the batteries also slide out and fit EGO's handheld tools, so a few packs can do double duty in a string trimmer or blower while the mower sits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electricity cost is the one place batteries win outright. A full charge of a 4 kWh kit at a residential rate of around 17 cents per kWh costs about 70 cents, plus charging losses. Even mowing weekly for 30 weeks, that is roughly $20 to $30 a season, against the gasoline, oil and filters a gas zero turn burns through.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Deck Size: 42, 52, 54 or 60 Inches
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a gas zero turn, deck width is mostly a time question. On an electric zero turn it is also a battery question, because a wider deck spins longer blades that draw more power. The manufacturers solve that by shipping bigger decks with bigger battery kits, which is why the 52-, 54- and 60-inch machines here all carry roughly 4 to 4.5 kWh while the 42-inch kits carry 2.2 to 2.5.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 42-inch deck suits lawns up to about 1.5 acres with trees, beds and gates. It fits through a standard 48-inch garden gate with a little room, it handles tight areas between landscaping, and it is what EGO's bagger kit and most of the electric accessories are built around. Our <a href="/best-42-inch-zero-turn-mower/">42-inch zero turn guide</a> covers the gas options at this width if you want a comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 52- or 54-inch deck is the sweet spot for 2 to 3.5 acres. It covers 24 to 29% more ground per pass than a 42-inch and still fits most 60-inch gates. On rolling or uneven lawns, the 54-inch Greenworks is a better choice than the 60-inch, because a narrower deck bridges fewer dips and scalps fewer high spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 60-inch deck only makes sense for open, flat acreage. It saves real time over three acres or more, but measure every gate and gap first. Overall machine width runs several inches wider than the deck, and electric mowers are not lighter than gas ones, so there is no getting it through a gap by lifting a corner.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
