        <div id="battery-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Battery Replacement Cost: The Number Nobody Puts on the Box
          </h2>

          <p>
            The batteries are the most expensive single component on an electric zero turn, and they wear out. Lithium-ion packs lose capacity with charge cycles, heat and age. Manufacturers do not publish cycle-life ratings for these mower kits, so nobody can tell you exactly when range drops to an annoying level, but it is reasonable to expect a noticeable decline over many seasons of weekly use, especially if the mower is stored in a hot shed or left fully charged all winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement prices are the part buyers underestimate. EGO's 56V 10.0Ah pack (BA5600T) has sold for about $549 at authorized resellers. The 12.0Ah pack (BA6720T) has been listed anywhere from about $659 to $942 depending on seller and timing. That puts a complete refresh of a ZT4204L's four packs around $2,200, and six packs for a ZT5207L somewhere between $4,000 and $5,600. Those numbers are why the warranty matters more than the horsepower claim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warranty and shared packs are the real value</h3>

          <p>
            Greenworks lists a 4-year limited warranty on the MaximusZ that covers both the mower and the batteries, and Cub Cadet lists a 4-year limited battery warranty on the ZT1 42E. Check the warranty terms for the exact kit you buy, including whether batteries are covered for residential use only and whether you need to register the purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            EGO's advantage is the platform. The Z6 packs are the same 56V batteries used in EGO's trimmers, blowers, chainsaws and push mowers, which we cover in our <a href="/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/">battery-powered lawn mower guide</a>. That means a pack that has faded too much for a full mow can still run a string trimmer for years, and it means you can buy one or two replacement packs at a time rather than replacing the whole set at once. Greenworks' 60V and 80V lines have the same idea but fewer tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party replacement packs do exist for some platforms at much lower prices. We would not put them in a ride-on mower. The battery management system on a riding mower is managing well over a kilowatt of draw, and a pack that fails under load or does not talk properly to the mower's controller is a fire and reliability risk you do not need on a $6,000 machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hills-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Hills, Weight and Steering
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric zero turns are not light. Spec listings put the EGO ZT4204L at about 432 pounds and the ZT5207L around 485, and Greenworks' bigger MaximusZ models are heavier still depending on the battery bundle. Most of that weight sits low in the batteries, which helps stability, but it also means soft spring turf takes ruts and a stuck mower is a two-person job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The slope rules for gas zero turns apply unchanged. Because the drive wheels do the steering, losing traction on a wet hill means losing steering at the same moment. Mow slopes up and down rather than across, stay well away from pond banks, ditches and retaining walls, and do not mow wet hillsides at all. Electric drive gives very smooth, fine throttle control, which owners commonly find makes slow work on a slope feel more controlled, but it does not add grip. Our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">mower for hills guide</a> covers what to buy if slopes are the main feature of your yard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Steering is where the ZT4215S is worth thinking about. Lap bars are intuitive after an afternoon for most people, but some buyers never get comfortable with them, and older owners with shoulder or grip problems sometimes find a wheel easier. EGO's e-Steer keeps the tight pivot of a zero turn while letting you drive with a wheel. It is a comfort feature, not a traction feature, so do not buy it expecting better slope behaviour.
          </p>

          <p>
            One battery-specific habit helps on hills: keep the charge level up. Climbing pulls the most current a mower ever draws, and a nearly empty pack under heavy load is when the controller is most likely to cut power to protect the cells. Do the steep sections early in the mow, not as the last job of the day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Buy Gas Instead, and the Models Not on Amazon
          </h2>

          <p>
            An electric zero turn is the right call for roughly one to three-and-a-half acres of mowed turf, mowed weekly, by an owner who hates engine maintenance and has a place to charge it. Outside that window the case weakens quickly. Over about four acres, even the largest kits here need perfect conditions to finish in one charge, and a gas machine refuels in minutes. If you only mow every two or three weeks, the extra grass length eats range. And if the mower lives outdoors under a tarp, the batteries will age faster than you want.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price is the other honest objection. A 42-inch electric zero turn with its batteries costs about as much as a well-built prosumer gas zero turn with a fabricated deck and serviceable transaxles. You are paying for silence, no fuel, and minimal maintenance, not for a tougher machine. If durability over 1,000-plus hours is the priority, a gas prosumer mower is still the better buy, and our <a href="/best-residential-zero-turn-mower/">residential zero turn guide</a> covers the strongest options in that price band.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Good electric zero turns you will not find on Amazon</h3>

          <p>
            Two notable electric zero turns are sold through other channels. The Cub Cadet ZT1 42E (56V, 3,000Wh battery, about 4 hours to charge, rated for up to 2 acres, about 6 mph) is sold through Cub Cadet dealers and home centers, and it is a strong pick if you want local dealer service. Ryobi's 80V HP 42-inch zero turn is a Home Depot product; reviews list about 2 acres of runtime and a fabricated 12-gauge deck. Neither appears in our ranked list because neither is sold through Amazon with a verifiable listing, but both are worth cross-shopping.
          </p>

          <p>
            EGO has also added newer 2026 Z6 kits, the ZT4214L 42-inch with six 6.0Ah packs (rated up to 1.5 acres) and the ZT5216L 52-inch with six 10.0Ah packs. Look at the battery count and amp-hours on whichever kit you buy, because the newer 42-inch kit actually ships with less total capacity than the ZT4204L: about 2.0 kWh against 2.2.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on lot size instead of mowed area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2-acre lot rarely has 2 acres of grass once you subtract the house, drive, beds and tree line. Measure the turf itself on a satellite map. Many buyers who think they need a 52-inch kit with six batteries are well served by a 42-inch kit with four, and save over $1,000.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the acreage rating in thick grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturer acreage figures are best cases measured under conditions nobody publishes. Damp spring growth and long grass can take a third or more off. Plan on about 70% of the rating, and if your lawn sits right on the line, buy the next battery kit up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging on a shared garage circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,600 watt charger draws about 13 amps, most of a 15-amp circuit. Share that circuit with a freezer or a shop vac and the breaker trips mid-charge, leaving you with a half-full mower in the morning. Give the charger its own circuit or a 20-amp outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the batteries hot or fully drained</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium packs age fastest in heat and at the extremes of charge. Do not leave the mower in a sun-baked shed all summer, and do not leave it dead or at 100% all winter. Store the packs indoors at a partial charge, as the manual specifies, to protect the most expensive part.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring battery replacement cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Six EGO 12.0Ah packs have been listed for $4,000 or more in total. Before buying, check the battery warranty term, whether it covers residential use, and whether you can replace one pack at a time. That cost decides whether the mower is cheaper than gas over ten years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric zero turn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most yards between 1.5 and 3 acres, the EGO Power+ Z6 ZT5207L. It ships with six 56V 12.0Ah batteries, about 4.0 kWh, and a 52-inch deck. Under 1.5 acres, the 42-inch ZT4204L costs less and fits through gates. Over 3 acres of open, flat ground, the Greenworks MZ606 with its 60-inch deck is the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many acres can an electric zero turn mow on one charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers rate the kits on this page from about 1.5 to over 4 acres per charge. Across these kits the ratings work out to roughly one kilowatt-hour of battery per acre. In thick or damp grass, owners commonly report less, so plan on about 70% of the published figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to charge a battery zero turn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Published figures range from about 2.5 to 4 hours with the included charger, depending on the kit. EGO and Greenworks both include 1.5 to 1.6 kW chargers that plug into a standard 120V outlet. The practical routine is to charge overnight and mow the next morning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much do replacement batteries cost for an electric zero turn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A lot. EGO's 56V 10.0Ah pack has sold for about $549 and the 12.0Ah pack for roughly $659 to $942. Replacing all six packs in a 52-inch EGO kit could cost $4,000 or more. Check the battery warranty before buying; Greenworks lists 4 years on the MaximusZ.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric zero turn mowers good on hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They handle the same slopes as gas zero turns, which is not very steep ones. Steering comes from the drive wheels, so losing traction means losing steering. Mow up and down, not across, never mow wet slopes, and do steep sections early while the battery is full, since climbing draws the most current.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an electric zero turn worth it over gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it if you mow 1 to 3.5 acres weekly, have a place to charge, and value no fuel, no oil changes and low noise. It is not worth it if you mow over four acres, mow infrequently so the grass grows long, or want maximum durability per dollar, where a prosumer gas zero turn still wins.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use EGO zero turn batteries in other EGO tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The Z6 uses standard EGO 56V ARC Lithium packs, the same ones that run EGO trimmers, blowers, chainsaws and push mowers. That is one of the platform's biggest advantages: a pack that has faded too much for a full mow can still power a handheld tool for years.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

